\begin_layout Section
Inequality in Denmark
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use two tables from Statistics Denmark,
 both based on 
\series bold
equivalised disposable income
\series default
,
 i.e.
 income after taxes and transfers,
 adjusted for household size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Gini coefficient and the top 10 percent share
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using table 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{IFOR41}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 from Statistics Denmark,
 produce a figure with the Gini coefficient for all of Denmark over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using table 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{IFOR32}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 add the share of total income going to the richest 10 percent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Describe how inequality in Denmark has developed since 1987.
 Do the two measures tell the same story?
 Compute the correlation between them over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint I
\bar default
:
 In 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
