\begin_layout Subsection
Prediction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fit a 
\series bold
linear
\series default
 and a 
\series bold
quadratic
\series default
 trend to the Gini coefficient,
 and use them to predict inequality in 2030 and 2040.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fitting a polynomial trend of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 by least squares means choosing the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=(\beta_{0},\dots,\beta_{p})$
\end_inset

 that solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\min_{\beta}\,S(\beta)=\sum_{i}\left(y_{i}-\sum^{p}_{k=0}\beta_{k}t^{k}_{i}\right)^{2},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an index for each observation with Gini coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}\in0,1,2,\dots$
\end_inset

,
 is the number of years since the start of the data set in 1987.
 The prediction for year 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum^{p}_{k=0}\beta_{k}\tau^{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Linear is 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1$
\end_inset

 and quadratic is 
\begin_inset Formula $p=2$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Solve this minimization problem yourself
\series default
 with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{scipy.optimize.minimize}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Check your answer
\series default
 against 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{np.polyfit}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Do you get the same coefficients?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use your estimates to predict the Gini coefficient in 2030 and 2040,
 and plot the result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How well do the two specifications fit the historical data?
 How much do their predictions differ?
 Would you trust either of them,
 and why?
\end_layout

