\begin_layout Section
Simulating the income distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now simulate an income distribution from a simple life-cycle model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Implement the model below in a documented
\series default
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{.py}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\series bold
module
\series default
 and import it into your notebook.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A cohort of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 individuals is followed from age 18 until retirement at age 65.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Education.

\series default
 At age 18 everybody is uneducated and outside the labor market.
 Each individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 draws an education 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\in\{\text{short},\text{medium},\text{long}\}$
\end_inset

 with probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 The education takes 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{e}$
\end_inset

 years,
 during which the individual receives a student grant 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{SU}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Labor market.

\series default
 After finishing their education,
 the individual enters the labor market as unemployed.
 Employment then follows a persistent two-state Markov chain:
 an unemployed person finds a job with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

,
 and an employed person loses their job with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Human capital.

\series default
 Individuals enter the labor market with education-specific human capital 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{e,0}$
\end_inset

.
 It then evolves as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h_{i,t+1}=\begin{cases}
h_{i,t}\left(1+\Delta_{e_{i}}\right)\psi_{i,t+1} & \text{if employed}\\
h_{i,t}\left(1-\delta\right)\psi_{i,t+1} & \text{if unemployed}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{e}$
\end_inset

 is an education-specific growth rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 is the depreciation rate while unemployed,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is a mean-one lognormal shock,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\log\psi\sim\mathcal{N}(-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{\psi},\sigma^{2}_{\psi})$
\end_inset

.
 Human capital is unchanged while in education.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint
\bar default
:
 Set up a random number generator with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and draw the shock with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{psi = rng.lognormal(-0.5*sigma_psi**2,
 sigma_psi,
 size=N)}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This is the same as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{psi = np.exp(rng.normal(-0.5*sigma_psi**2,
 sigma_psi,
 size=N))}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 -- note that the two arguments are the mean and the standard deviation of the 
\emph on
underlying normal
\emph default
,
 not of the lognormal itself.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{\psi}$
\end_inset

 is what makes the mean exactly one:
 without it the mean would be 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\sigma^{2}_{\psi}/2)$
\end_inset

.
 Check it with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
lstinline{psi.mean()}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Income.
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i,t}=\begin{cases}
y^{SU} & \text{in education}\\
h_{i,t} & \text{if employed}\\
\rho y^{\text{last job}}_{i} & \text{if unemployed}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
i.e.
 an unemployed person receives the replacement rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 of the income in the 
\emph on
previous
\emph default
 job.
 Use a floor 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{y}$
\end_inset

 for those who have never been employed.
\end_layout

