\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Compute the Gini coefficient
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Write your own function that computes the Gini coefficient of a vector of incomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint
\bar default
:
 Test it on cases where you know the answer.
 The Gini coefficient of a uniform distribution on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

 is exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $1/3$
\end_inset

,
 and of a lognormal distribution whose logarithm has standard deviation 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 it is 
\begin_inset Formula $2\Phi(s/\sqrt{2})-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute the Gini coefficient for the full simulated sample.
 Plot the Lorenz curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute it separately for each age.
 How does inequality within an age group compare with inequality when all ages are pooled?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What drives inequality?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Run a series of alternative simulations,
 where the components of the model are switched off one at a time:
 educational differences,
 shocks to human capital,
 depreciation of human capital while unemployed,
 and unemployment itself.
 Report the Gini coefficient for each simulation and compare it to the baseline.
 Which mechanism matters the most?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Hint
\bar default
:
 Report the Gini coefficient both for all ages pooled and within a single age group.
 They need not move in the same direction,
 and where they do not,
 you should explain why.
\end_layout

